	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The outcome was time from lung transplantation to death or retransplantation, evaluated at 1, 5, and 10 years. In the temporal testing cohort, the 1-year time-dependent AUC was 0.61 (95% CI 0.52–0.70). The testing-set integrated AUC was 0.61 (95% CI 0.60–0.63) and C-index was 0.64 (95% CI 0.63–0.64). Accuracy, PPV, sensitivity, specificity, NPV, and F1-score were not reported. Calibration was assessed using plots, calibration slope, observed-to-expected ratios, and Brier scores; decision-curve analysis showed net benefit across clinically relevant thresholds.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study used a large national registry and transparently identified all adult first-time lung transplant recipients during the specified period. Exclusions for duplicates, age younger than 18 years, unknown follow-up, and index-hospitalization death or retransplantation were explicitly reported in the participant flowchart and were broadly consistent with the stated postdischarge prediction setting.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The model included hospital length of stay and posttransplant ventilation support while survival time was measured from the date of transplantation. These predictors accrue after time zero and can reflect early complications or impending death; the authors note that very short stays may represent “early, catastrophic events,” creating temporal leakage, reverse causation, and potential immortal-time bias.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Death or retransplantation is an objective time-to-event outcome recorded consistently in the national registry, and the 1-, 5-, and 10-year horizons were prespecified. Index-hospitalization events were excluded to align the model with postdischarge use.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The large sample, prespecified temporal split, validation cohort, automated variable selection, confidence intervals, calibration, and DCA were strengths. However, the “final model was obtained via recalibration-in-the-large on the 2015 to 2025 cohort,” and calibration and clinical-utility performance were then reported in that same testing cohort. This means the temporal test set was not fully independent for all reported performance measures and may yield optimistic calibration and DCA estimates.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates an interpretable machine-learning–assisted model for death or retransplantation after lung transplantation using national registry data. It is directly applicable to AI prediction research in thoracic organ transplantation, although its intended use is postdischarge rather than pretransplant candidate selection.

